\details{
Overlays the coefficients of a linear model onto a \code{ggformula} plot so
students can see what each \code{b} in the model equation \emph{is} on the graph. The
intercept \code{b0} is marked, and every other coefficient is drawn as an arrow
showing its size and direction:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Categorical predictor} -- \code{b0} is a horizontal line at the reference
group's mean, and each \code{b_k} is an arrow from that line to group \code{k}'s mean.
\item \strong{Continuous predictor} -- \code{b1} is drawn as a rise-over-run right triangle
(the slope), and \code{b0} as a hollow dot where the line crosses \code{x = 0}.
}

\code{gf_b()} is the name used most in teaching; \code{gf_coef()} is a fully supported
alias for it.

Best on a small sample so the arrows and labels do not crowd the points. For
the continuous case the "run" of the slope triangle is auto-selected as a
round number near 10\% of the x span; pass \code{run} to set it yourself (e.g.
\code{run = 1} to show the slope per one-unit change).
}
